 their ECL mechanism and constant ECL responses were investigated.
Currently, the resonance energy transfer (RET) strategy with the merits of low background signal and high sensitivity has been extensively investigated in ECL sensors.19−21 It is well-known that when the luminophores donor is close to the acceptor, the RET system can give rise to the quench of the donor’s emission in the way of nonradiative energy dissipation. The perfect spectral overlapping between the ECL spectrum and absorption spectrum of donor and acceptor, respectively, is critical for ECL-RET system.20 The ECL spectra of BP NSs was recorded as shown in curve a of Figure S1, where a strong ECL emission at ${510}\mathrm{nm}$ was observed. The UV−vis spectra of Au NPs with an average size of $20~\mathrm{{nm}}$ displayed a significant absorption in the range of $450{-}600~\mathrm{nm}$ which was overlapped perfectly with the ECL emission of BP NSs, implying the possibility of the ECL-RET strategy between BP NSs and Au NPs.
On the other hand, commonly, an expensive electrochemical workstation is an indispensable element to supply voltage and collect the detection signals, which makes the paper-based analytical devices complicated and depart from the trends of portability and low cost.22 Over the past few years, remarkable efforts have been invested in developing alternative portable power sources to replace the electrochemical workstation in ECL sensors.23,24 The low-cost rechargeable battery was selected to construct a portable battery-triggered ECL sensor for multiple tumor markers detection.25 The hand-held paperbased bipolar electrode ECL system with luminol/ $\mathrm{^{\prime}H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ as the luminophore driven by a rechargeable battery was also designed.23 Although the rechargeable battery with the advantage of low-cost, its portability and energy storage capacity is limited and the repeated charging process restricts its long-term working. Therefore, developing a new power source is highly desired. Perovskite solar cells (PSCs), which can convert inexhaustible and clean solar energy into electrical energy, have obtained great attention mainly arising from their facile fabrication, low-cost, and high photoelectric conversion efficiency.26−29 These superior features make PSCs capable of serving as the power source to replace the electrochemical workstation to drive the ECL reaction. The previously works have been reported that using the PSC device as the power source to drive the fluorescent electrochromic windows and photoelectrochemical cells.30,31 Nevertheless, until now, PSCs have not been explored in ECL bioanalysis. Recently, we have designed a portable PSC on the cellulose paper substrate,32 which is particularly suitable to combine with a paper-based ECL sensing platform serving as the portable power source. The design, fabrication procedures, and characterizations of the paper-based PSC power source are shown in the Supporting Information (Figures S2−S5).
Besides that, in order to further enhance the analytical performance, duplex specific nuclease (DSN) which is able to specifically cleave the DNA in DNA−RNA heteroduplexes without cleaving the single-stranded DNA or RNA,33 is also introduced to implement the signal amplification strategy. Herein, a paper-based PSC driven ECL-RET sensing platform with efficient BP NSs luminophore and Au NPs as a donor− acceptor system is constructed based on the DSN-assisted target recycling signal amplification strategy. As exhibited in Scheme 1, when the DNA hairpins conjugated with Au NPs are closely located at the surface of BP NSs, the energy transfer from BP NSs to Au NPs occurs. In that case, the ECL emission of BP NSs is off. When the DSN nuclease and target analyte are present, the specific DNA−RNA binding and DSN enzyme cleaving could trigger the detachment of Au NPs from the BP NSs surface. Therefore, the ECL emission of BP NSs is recovered. In addition, the DSN enzyme can cleave the formed DNA−RNA binding and release the miRNA-107, which can further open the other DNA hairpins. Then the newly formed DNA−RNA binding can also be cleaved by the DSN enzyme, achieving the target recycling signal amplification strategy. In that case, even with very low concentrations of the target, an acceptable ECL signal can be obtained. In this way, the fabricated sensing platform could sensitively and selectively detect miRNA-107 through monitoring the variations of ECL intensity. The details about the preparation of BP NSs, aminomodified BP NSs, Au NPs, and Au NPs−DNA composite are shown in the Supporting Information.
The design and assay procedures of the paper-based constant potential ECL-RET sensing platform are shown in the Supporting Information (Figure S6). The NMP solvent exfoliation method was adopted to obtain the BP NSs. Figure 1a illustrates the scanning electron microscopy (SEM) of the
BP NSs sample, which highlighted their 2D nanosheet structure. The size distribution of obtained BP NSs was provided by dynamic light scattering spectroscopy, and most of the NSs size were $300{-}400~\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure S7). Further evidence of the 2D structure of BP NSs could also be seen in the transmission electron microscope (TEM) image. As displayed in Figure 1b, the typical lamellar structure containing multilayer nanosheet stacking was observed, corresponding to the SEM result. The clear fringes with the lattice spacing of $0.34\mathrm{nm}$ in the high-resolution TEM image was assigned to the (021) plane of BP crystal (Figure 1c). Moreover, the X-ray diffraction (XRD) pattern was shown in Figure 1d, where all of the diffraction peaks could be indexed to the orthorhombic phase (JCPDS No. 73-1358), confirming the great crystallinity of BP NSs.34 The Raman spectra in Figure 1e also suggested the great crystallinity. The optical behavior of BP NSs was evaluated using UV−vis absorbance as shown in Figure 1f. A broad absorption from UV to the NIR region was observed, implying a more sufficient utilization of solar energ y.35,36 What’s more, light yellow color was observed when BP NSs dispersed in the NMP solvent (left inset in Figure 1f). While excitation with a UV lamp, the BP NSs exhibited bright green emission (right inset in Figure 1f). The BP NSs can also emit fluorescence at $510\ \mathrm{nm}$ as shown in Figure S8.
