The ECL intensity reached the maximum when the potential was around $-1.5\mathrm{\DeltaV}$ . Thus, $-1.5\mathrm{\DeltaV}$ was selected as the optimal potential. Besides, to assess the feasibility of the proposed constant ECL sensing strategy, Figure 2c gives the ECL response of BP NSs with (black curve) and without (red curve) ${S_{2}\mathrm{O}_{8}}^{2-}$ under the potential supply of two series PSC devices. As seen, with the voltage on and off, the BP NSs exhibited a sensitive and small ECL response. While, when with ${S_{2}}{\mathrm{O}}_{8}^{2-},$ a significant large ECL intensity was observed, implying the critical role of $S_{2}\mathrm{\bar{O}}_{8}^{2-}$ in the emission process and the promising constant ECL application of BP NSs. Importantly, even without the $S_{2}\bar{\mathrm{O}_{8}}^{2-}$ coreactant, a small ECL response of BP NSs can also be observed, which may be that the $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ in the solution acted as the coreactant. While, when in the ${\bf N}_{2}$ -saturated solution, the pure BP NSs showed a negligible ECL signal (blue curves in Figure $^{2\mathrm{b,c}},$ ).
Ultimately, the analytical performance of the ECL-RET sensing platform was investigated by incubation with different concentration of miRNA-107. Clearly, the obtained ECL intensity enhanced along with the increase of the concentration of miRNA-107, as shown in Figure 3a. Figure 3b exhibited that the ECL intensity of the sensing platform was linear to the logarithm of the target concentrations from 0.1 $\mathrm{{\ttpM}}$ to $15~\mathrm{nM}$ with the regression equation of $I=1144\log c+$ 1110 (where I stands for the obtained ECL intensity) and the limit of detection was $0.04\mathrm{pM}$ , with a correlation coefficient of 0.997. Such a desired linear range was attributed to the efficient target recycling signal amplification strategy.
In order to evaluate the specificity of the designed ECL sensing platform, $100\ \mathrm{pg\mL^{-1}}$ of miRNA-21 and miRNA-141 were selected as the interfering substances and their sequence information were shown in Table S1. As shown in Figure 3c, the ECL intensities were almost the same and much lower than that of the miRNA-107 analyte when incubated with the above mentioned interferences, suggesting the acceptable selectivity of the sensing platform. The reproducibility was assessed with interassay precision between 10 devices. The relative standard deviation for the parallel detection of 10, 100, and ${500}~\mathrm{pM}$ miRNA-107 were $3.12\%$ , $3.69\%,$ , and $4.21\%$ , respectively, implying satisfactory reproducibility of the sensing platform. In addition, the stability was also investigated by examining the ECL response with the voltage switched on/off for $500\ \mathrm{~s~}$ (Figure 3d). As seen, the ECL signal showed excellent stability with the miRNA-107 concentrations of 1.0 and $100\ \mathrm{pM}$ and there was nearly no change, implying the acceptable stability.
In conclusion, a PSC powered paper-based constant potential ECL-RET sensing platform was designed for miRNA-107 detection with BP NSs as the luminophore. The excellent photoelectric conversion performance and low-cost synthesis makes PSCs promising candidates for the nextgeneration power source of ECL sensors, implying the great application potential of PSC in the field of bioanalysis. Thanks to the overlapping of spectra, an ECL-RET sensing platform was constructed based on BP NSs and Au NPs. What’s more, DSN was also introduced to achieve the target recycling signal amplification strategy. Most importantly, this work not only first explored the ECL behavior of BP NSs for ECL sensing platform construction but also offered a good example for the application of a PSC device in bioanalysis.
